9share -- share directories between machines over 9P, through ssh

9share makes a directory on one machine appear at a mount point on another, through ssh.  The
machine with the files dials out, so the other needs no open port and no server to configure,
and a connection that drops is picked up again where it stopped.  Install the same package on
both machines; nothing happens on a machine until something is configured on it.

Fast path

    Needs 9share on both machines, and ssh from the first to the second without a password
    (ssh HOST true).  On the machine that has the files, as yourself:

        9share export /srv/projects bob.example.org

    On bob.example.org, once, as root (the line above prints it, ready to copy):

        sudo 9share mount alice projects /mnt/projects

    /srv/projects now appears at /mnt/projects, and stays there.  alice is the account on
    bob.example.org that ssh logs in as; projects is the share's name, by default the
    directory's own.  To be asked instead of typing these, run 9share setup on either machine:
    three questions, the same result.

That is all of it.  The rest is optional:

    9share ro projects        read-only (as that account, no sudo)
    9share rw projects        read-write
    9share off projects       not mounted
    9share reload             read the configuration again (the commands do it for
                              themselves; this is for a hand-edited file)
    9share status             what is shared, what is mounted, what is running
    9share pair projects bob.example.org
                              make a secret on both machines, so that each connection
                              proves it as well as trusting ssh (needs 9share on bob)
    9share unexport projects  stop sharing
    9share import bob.example.org projects
                              the other way round, when bob cannot be dialled: show
                              bob's directory here (this machine runs ssh; the same
                              sudo 9share mount here, then on both machines 9share)

Several directories, several machines, and both directions at once are each one more block
of configuration.  The configuration is two small files in obconf, the notation of the
OpenBSD daemons' config files (9share.conf(5)); the commands write it and you may edit it
by hand:

    export "projects" {             # ~/.config/9share/9share.conf, where the files are
        directory /srv/projects
        host bob.example.org
    }

    mount "projects" {              # /etc/9share/9share.conf, where they are shown
        user alice
        at /mnt/projects
    }

Installing: the package finds out how the machine starts services and starts the daemon at
boot that way: systemd, WSL2 (with or without systemd), sysvinit, OpenRC.  Where it cannot
(a container, runit) it says what to run.  It starts with nothing shared, a commented
template in /etc/9share/9share.conf, and a message to run 9share setup.

How it works: 9share runs daud, a 9P2000.L file server (a fork of diod, in this package), as
you, unprivileged.  daud runs ssh to the other machine, where 9share link joins ssh to a
relay of the mount's user; the 9P connection rides a resumable link over that, so when ssh
drops and is run again, each end resends the bytes the other has not acknowledged and the
connection, with its open files, never notices.  The root daemon there mounts it with the
kernel's 9p client.  A mount waits for a lost connection for up to its hold time (600
seconds unless set), then is removed, and comes back when the exporter does.

Speed: the relay understands 9P.  When a file is read in sequence it asks daud for the following
pieces ahead of time (up to 64 requests and 2 MiB in flight, as scp does) and answers the kernel
from what has arrived, so the round trip of the link no longer sets the speed.  A write, a change
of attributes, a seek or three seconds discards what was read ahead.  `sudo 9share readahead
[off|on|KB]` changes it while running; `sudo 9share shutdown` and `sudo 9share restart` stop and
restart the daemon cleanly.

Requirements: Linux with the 9p module (Debian, Ubuntu, WSL2 and others), openssh-client,
and key-based ssh from the machine with the files to the one that shows them.
To build from source: a C compiler, autoconf, automake and autoconf-archive.

Security: the export is the directory you name, served with your rights; export only what the
account that mounts it may see.  The mount is nosuid,nodev, and only the account named in
`9share mount` can use it: the kernel refuses every other user of the receiving machine, root
included (anyone who can become that account can, of course).  To open a mount to every local user on
purpose, the administrator gives it `access any` in /etc/9share/9share.conf.  The relay takes a connection only
from a process of that account and, once you have run 9share pair, only one that proves the shared
secret (SCRAM-SHA-256), so nothing can pose as the exporter without the secret.  The secret does not
encrypt anything; ssh does.  The configuration files are used only if no one but their owner (or root) could
have changed them (not writable by group or others, in a directory likewise); a file that is too open is
refused, and so are the commands that would edit it.

See INSTALL, HACKING, 9share(8), 9share-setup(1), 9share.conf(5), daud(8).
License: GPL-2.0-or-later for 9share; daud is diod's licence (GPL-2, with libnpfs MIT-like), see COPYING and daud/COPYING.
